KNOWNBUG: std::string fill-construction std::string(n, c) is a no-op.

The fill constructor dispatches to basic_string<char>::_M_construct(
size_type, char), which for the explicitly-instantiated basic_string<char>
(extern template) is realised as a declaration only: its body is never
instantiated and is discarded by cpp_typecheckt::clean_up().  Both the length
and the character contents are therefore left wrong (size() == 3 already
FAILS today, in addition to s[0]).

Same underlying gap as cpp11_string_literal_char_access: the elaborate-only
realisation path basic_string<char> takes does not convert deferred inline
member bodies.  Grounded in N5008 [temp.inst]/4 and Note 4.  See
doc/architectural/cpp-extern-template-member-instantiation.md.

When the extern-template member-instantiation fix lands, both assertions
become SUCCESS and verification succeeds; reclassify to CORE.
